# BLOG 1025: React Testing Library Best Practices
# ============================================================
safe_blog(
    "React Testing Library Best Practices for Reliable Tests",
    "react-testing-library-best-practices-reliable-tests",
    "2023-03-15",
    "Write better React tests with Testing Library: queries, user events, async patterns, accessibility-first testing, and common mistakes.",
    ["Testing", "React", "Testing Library", "JavaScript"],
    "testing",
    [
        ("The Guiding Philosophy: Test Like a User",
         "React Testing Library (RTL) embodies a radical philosophy: your tests should resemble how users interact with your software. This means no testing implementation details like component state, internal methods, or lifecycle hooks. Instead, render components and interact with them through the same interfaces users use — clicking buttons, filling forms, reading text, and waiting for content to appear. Tests written this way survive refactors because they don't depend on internal structure.\n\nKent C. Dodds created Testing Library as a response to Enzyme's approach of testing component internals. Enzyme tests that assert on state values or shallow rendering output break whenever you refactor component implementation, even when behavior is identical. Testing Library queries elements the way users find them: by role, label text, placeholder text, or visible text content. When you rename a CSS class or move state from useState to useReducer, these tests pass unchanged because the rendered output didn't change.\n\nThe guiding principle is: the more your tests resemble the way your software is used, the more confidence they give you. This philosophy extends to every decision: prefer getByRole over getByTestId because screen readers use roles; prefer userEvent over fireEvent because userEvent simulates real keyboard and mouse behavior; prefer findBy over waitFor because findBy expresses intent more clearly."),
        ("Query Priority: Choosing the Right Selector",
         "Testing Library provides a priority order for queries, from most preferred to least. The top priority — getByRole — finds elements by their ARIA role, which is how assistive technology identifies them. getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit' }) finds a button with the text 'Submit', matching how a screen reader presents it. This catches accessibility issues automatically: if a div pretends to be a button without the button role, getByRole won't find it.\n\ngetByLabelText pairs form inputs with their labels, testing the actual form structure users interact with. getByPlaceholderText works for inputs with placeholder text, though placeholders shouldn't replace labels for accessibility. getByText finds non-interactive elements by their visible text content. getByDisplayValue finds form elements by their current value, useful after user interaction.\n\nThe lower-priority queries — getByAltText for images, getByTitle for title attributes, getByTestId for data-testid attributes — should be used only when higher-priority queries aren't applicable. The getByTestId escape hatch is valuable for elements with no accessible role, label, or text (like wrapper divs for styling), but overusing it signals potential accessibility problems. If you can't find an element with getByRole, ask whether the element should have an ARIA role or label."),
        ("User Events: Simulating Real Interactions",
         "Testing Library provides two event APIs: fireEvent (low-level DOM event dispatch) and userEvent (high-level user interaction simulation). Always prefer userEvent for its realistic behavior simulation. When a user types into an input, they press keys one at a time, triggering keydown, keypress, input, and keyup events. userEvent.type() simulates this complete sequence; fireEvent.change() only dispatches a synthetic change event that doesn't match real user behavior.\n\nThe @testing-library/user-event library (version 14+) uses a setup pattern: const user = userEvent.setup(); then await user.click(element), await user.type(input, 'text'), await user.keyboard('{Enter}'). The setup function accepts options like delay (for realistic typing speed), advanceTimers (for fake timer integration), and pointerEventsCheck (for pointer event validation).\n\nCommon interaction patterns include: filling and submitting forms (type into inputs, click submit), keyboard navigation (tab through elements, activate with Enter/Space), drag and drop (using pointer events), clipboard operations (copy/paste), and file uploads (using upload() with File objects). Each pattern requires the corresponding userEvent method — using fireEvent directly for complex interactions leads to incomplete event sequences that mask bugs."),
        ("Async Testing: findBy and waitFor",
         "Asynchronous UI updates — loading spinners, API responses, animations — require async testing patterns. Testing Library provides findBy queries (which retry until an element appears) and waitFor (which retries arbitrary assertions). findByRole, findByText, findByTestId return promises that resolve when the element is found within the timeout, or reject if it never appears.\n\nThe preferred pattern is: const element = await screen.findByText('Data loaded'); expect(element).toBeInTheDocument(). This clearly expresses 'wait for this text to appear, then verify it's there.' The findBy queries default to a 1000ms timeout, configurable globally via configure({ asyncUtilTimeout: 5000 }) or per-query with the timeout option.\n\nwaitFor wraps any assertion in a retry loop: await waitFor(() => { expect(mockApi).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1) }). Use waitFor when you need to assert on something that isn't an element query — like mock function calls, URL changes, or console output. Never wrap a findBy in waitFor (it already retries) and never use waitForElementToBeRemoved without ensuring the element exists first. These common mistakes lead to confusing test failures."),
        ("Testing Forms, Modals, and Complex Interactions",
         "Form testing follows the user flow: render the form, fill inputs, submit, and assert on the result. Use userEvent.type() for text inputs, userEvent.selectOptions() for dropdowns, userEvent.click() for checkboxes and radio buttons, and userEvent.upload() for file inputs. After submission, assert on either the success message, the submitted data (captured through a mock function), or the validation error messages.\n\nModal testing requires handling focus management and portal rendering. When a modal opens, focus should move to the modal content. Test this with expect(modalContent).toHaveFocus(). When the modal closes, focus should return to the trigger element. Test keyboard interaction: Escape should close the modal, Tab should trap focus within the modal. These accessibility behaviors are critical to test because screen reader users depend on them.\n\nComplex interactions like drag-and-drop, multi-step wizards, and real-time collaboration require careful sequencing of user events and async assertions. For multi-step forms, test each step's validation before proceeding. For real-time features, mock the WebSocket or SSE connection and simulate incoming messages. The key principle remains: simulate exactly what a user would do, in the order they would do it, and assert on what they would see."),
        ("Mocking in Testing Library Tests",
         "Mock API calls using msw (Mock Service Worker) for the most realistic testing approach. MSW intercepts requests at the network level, meaning your application code makes real fetch/axios calls that get intercepted and responded to with mock data. This tests the complete request/response cycle including headers, error handling, and serialization.\n\nSet up MSW with a handlers file defining request interceptors: rest.get('/api/users', (req, res, ctx) => res(ctx.json(mockUsers))). In your test setup, start the MSW server before tests and reset handlers between tests. For component tests, use setupWorker for browser-based interception; for Node tests (jest/vitest), use setupServer.\n\nMock module dependencies with jest.mock() or vi.mock() for external libraries like analytics, logging, or third-party SDKs. Mock window.location, window.open, and navigator.clipboard for testing browser API interactions. The @testing-library/jest-dom package adds DOM-specific matchers like toHaveTextContent(), toHaveAttribute(), toHaveClass(), and toBeVisible() that make element assertions expressive and readable."),
        ("Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them",
         "The most common mistake is testing implementation details through state assertions. Don't write expect(component.state.count).toBe(1) — instead, assert on the rendered output: expect(screen.getByText('Count: 1')).toBeInTheDocument(). If you find yourself reaching into component internals, reconsider what behavior you're actually trying to verify.\n\nUsing waitFor excessively signals potential issues. If every test needs waitFor wrapping, your components may be making too many sequential state updates or your mock setup isn't properly controlling async behavior. Fix the root cause rather than plastering waitFor everywhere. Properly mock APIs, use act() for state updates outside user events, and ensure your test environment supports the async patterns your components use.\n\nSkipping cleanup leads to test interdependence — tests that only pass when run in a specific order. Testing Library automatically unmounts components after each test when using the cleanup function (enabled by default in most test runners). If you're experiencing test pollution, verify that global state (window, document, localStorage) is reset between tests and that mock functions are cleared in beforeEach blocks."),
    ]
)

